WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.890
A fan-favorite theater troupe, a scene that
explains the title, and a hair metal power

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.710
fantasy all kids can relate to.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.500
"Thor: Love and Thunder" is one bombastic
Marvel movie, but these are the scenes that

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.410
most deserve a second watch.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.339
Warning: major spoilers ahead.

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:18.470
The opening to "Thor: Love and Thunder" is
interesting for how different it starts from

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.930
the usual Marvel movies.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.390
For one, it is practically silent at the beginning,
and there's no Marvel logo or fanfare.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.800
The movie simply begins.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.480
It's a disorienting shot of the desert.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.940
As the camera rises and turns, we meet Christian
Bale as Gorr.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.539
Just like in the comics that inspired the
film, Gorr is just a desperate father seeking

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:37.679
help from his god to save his child.

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:41.039
It's uncharacteristically somber and does
an excellent job of pulling you in.

00:00:41.039 --> 00:00:45.800
It also sets up a strange connection between
this film and "Star Trek V: The Final Frontier."

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.649
That film also opens with a weathered bald
man in the desert.

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:52.289
Also, Gorr's goal is to reach the center of
the universe and have his wish granted by

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:54.089
the entity referred to as Eternity.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:58.239
That sounds suspiciously like Spock's half-brother
Sybok's plan to meet God at the center of

00:00:58.239 --> 00:00:59.239
the galaxy.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:00.239
Coincidence?

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:01.239
Perhaps.

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:04.809
Watch both openings again, though, and you
might see the connection.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:08.190
All the high-flying, wacky adventure stuff
you would expect from a Guardians of the Galaxy

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.710
and Thor team-up occurs after Gorr's origin
and a quick rundown of the previous film.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:16.170
We learn that Thor is done wandering around
the cosmos looking for people to save.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.330
Instead, he will sit and wait for someone
to say that they need his help to win this

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.330
battle.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.950
"Well, you know what they say, better late
than not at all."

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.540
"That's very nice."

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.780
Peter Quill says those exact words, and Thor
rides on his ax Stormbreaker like the Wicked

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.700
Witch in "The Wizard of Oz" before single-handedly
defeating the enemy.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.430
It is a bonkers sequence filled with humor
and incredible action.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:40.820
It's got lasers, Guns N' Roses, Jim Henson-esque
creatures laughing and firing at Thor, a Jean-Claude

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.740
Van Damme split, and it's a lot of fun.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.410
Watching this sequence is like seeing every
'80s and early '90s nerd's dream of what a

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.280
"Thor" movie should be come to life.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.980
It isn't particularly insightful about the
human condition, and it doesn't break a lot

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.610
of new ground regarding superhero action,
but it's an absolute blast.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:01.060
Why wouldn't you want to relive such a wild
and invigorating sequence more than once?

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.850
Jane Foster isn't holding up so well in "Thor:
Love and Thunder."

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.550
She's still a brilliant scientist using her
intelligence to try and save the world, but

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.060
she has also been diagnosed with stage IV
cancer.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.799
Her old friends Darcy and Selvig are the only
ones who know about her illness, and Darcy

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:15.709
thinks Jane should reach out to Thor.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:19.409
While Jane doesn't want to do that, Thor's
broken hammer Mjolnir is calling out to her.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:22.319
Jane travels to New Asgard in Norway to find
the hammer.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:25.890
While she's there, Asgardian actors, just
like in "Thor: Ragnarok," are performing a

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.620
scene from the previous movie.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.989
They're doing Odin's death scene and using
every cheap stage production trick they can

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:32.319
think of to pull it off.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:36.389
The scene is worth a second viewing because
it is hilarious, especially when Melissa McCarthy

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:41.340
arrives as Hela, and because it reminds viewers
that when an Asagardian dies and enters Valhalla,

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.090
they disappear in wisps of gold.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.069
This time, you can take in the funny performances
and appreciate the foreshadowing of what will

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:48.799
happen in the final act with Jane.

00:02:48.799 --> 00:02:53.420
Thanks to the trailers, it comes as no surprise
that Natalie Portman's Jane Foster would be

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.299
wielding the power of Thor in the film.

00:02:55.299 --> 00:02:58.749
However, the anticipation for this moment
is so intense and the scene itself has so

00:02:58.749 --> 00:03:04.480
much going on that when it finally happens,
you're thrilled.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.879
"Jane?"

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:08.499
The acceleration of the sequence is what really
stands out.

00:03:08.499 --> 00:03:11.139
It begins with Gorr arriving at New Asgard.

00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:15.129
Using his new powers, he creates shadow creatures
that eerily stretch all along the Asgardian

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:16.129
streets.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:19.819
When they start attacking, Thor, Korg, and
King Valkyrie are there to fight them off.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:22.239
During the battle, Thor hears a familiar sound.

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:25.690
Looking around the chaos of battle, he sees
his reconstructed Mjolnir.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:29.260
Just as the hammer is about to reach his hand,
it flies back to its current master: Jane

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.260
Foster.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.239
Things turn into a violent and epic romantic
comedy as the two banter a bit before Gorr

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:35.560
goes on the attack.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:39.110
Then we learn this was all a ruse for Gorr
to capture the children and hide them in the

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.110
Shadow Realm.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.750
The seamless tonal shifts are very impressive,
and the moment Jane holds Mjolnir is a stand

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.010
up and clap moment.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:49.290
With Thanos, Marvel created someone who was
multidimensional with a point of view you

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.510
could understand, even though you probably
don't agree with him.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.909
They've done this again in "Thor: Love and
Thunder" in the form of Gorr the God Butcher.

00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:59.749
From his first appearance, you understand
why he is so angry and vengeful.

00:03:59.749 --> 00:04:03.129
He was clearly a devoted disciple of a god
who betrayed him with indifference.

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:07.799
That would be enough for us to understand
him, but we empathize because he is so complicated.

00:04:07.799 --> 00:04:10.140
On a visual level, he looks haunted and creepy.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.790
However, Christian Bale's voice isn't heavy,
deep, or raspy.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.030
In a few scenes, his voice is high-pitched,
almost wounded.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:20.160
For instance, take the scene in which he emerges
from the shadows, intentionally terrifying

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.870
the children he's captured by producing a
creature and ripping its head off in a horrifying

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:24.870
display of dominance.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.080
He doesn't smile, he doesn't laugh.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.690
Instead, he looks sad, tired, and confused.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.030
It's worth watching again for the shock of
how dark the moment is and to savor the nuances

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:35.970
of the performance.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:40.289
Since Gorr exists in the Shadow Realm, Thor
and friends need to borrow Zeus' weapon, Thunderbolt,

00:04:40.289 --> 00:04:41.570
to fight the darkness.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:45.680
This brings them to the incredible Omnipotence
City where all the gods reside.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.110
Everything about this place deserves a rewatch
because it is so beautiful and packed with

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.639
Easter eggs and references that only multiple
viewings can fully reveal.

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:56.509
"Let's see who you are and take off your disguise."

00:04:56.509 --> 00:04:58.930
The star of the show, though, is Russell Crowe
as Zeus.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.860
To call his performance over the top would
be an understatement.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.900
He is devouring the scenery around him like
an all-you-can-eat buffet and clearly loving

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.080
every second of it.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.060
In a film this wacky, though, it absolutely
works, and Crowe is an utter delight.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.520
During their visit to Omnipotence City, our
heroes discover that Gorr's goal is to make

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.020
it to the center of the universe in order
to reach a being called Eternity.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.729
The legend says that the first person to reach
Eternity will have their wish granted.

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:26.270
Since Gorr's title is God Butcher, Valkyrie
implies that his wish would be to wipe all

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.560
gods out of existence.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.620
So, they journey to the Shadow Realm to save
the children and stop Gorr from getting his

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.620
wish.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.800
In a quest story, this would usually be the
part where everything slows down and the audience

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.150
gets a little bored waiting for something
to happen.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:42.600
"Thor: Love and Thunder" keeps things interesting
by giving us cosmic vistas with space dolphins,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.050
some world-building regarding the reproductive
process of Korg's species, and a warm heart-to-heart

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:49.050
between Thor and Jane.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:53.370
Upon arriving in the Shadow Realm, all the
color drains out of the film, except for Thunderbolt,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.970
which still crackles and sizzles with Zeus'
power.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:59.580
The ominous nature of this mysterious place
sets in right away, and Thor's friends are

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.020
on the lookout for an ambush or any signs
of the children they're looking for.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.449
While journeying through the tunnels deep
within the dark moon, Jane uncovers Gorr's

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:07.449
plan.

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:11.349
He needs Stormbreaker to conjure the Bifrost
in order to open the gates to Eternity.

00:06:11.349 --> 00:06:15.069
Which kind of makes sense considering how
powerful the bridge is according to Heimdall.

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:20.120
"To leave the bridge open would unleash the
full power of the Bifrost and destroy Jotunheim

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.319
with you upon it."

00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:26.409
The fact that kidnapping the Asgardian children
was all a ruse isn't surprising, but that's

00:06:26.409 --> 00:06:27.780
not the point of the scene.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.729
The reason this moment is so powerful is witnessing
just how brutal Gorr can be.

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:34.500
He moves through the shadows like a reanimated
corpse.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.750
He speaks through sharp, rotten teeth, and
even tortures Thor to make him call his ax.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:42.180
Some viewers may not wish to relive such an
aggressively dark scene, but the strength

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.060
of the performances should be enough to bring
you back.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.539
There was a time when the primary audience
for superhero stories was kids.

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:51.490
Adult fans may not like to admit this fact,
but it's true.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:55.159
As new creators entered the comic book industry,
they started updating their stories to reflect

00:06:55.159 --> 00:06:59.090
their interests and, eventually, superhero
comics matured to a point where most parents

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:02.860
probably wouldn't allow their kids to read
them until reaching some mutually agreed-upon

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:03.860
age.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.900
We have lived in this paradigm for so long
that it's easy to forget the more innocent

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.740
origins of our favorite characters.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:12.680
Family and kid-friendly comics still exist,
of course, but the vast majority of superhero

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.199
films are aimed at older audiences.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:18.590
That is why it is so rewarding when Thor shares
his powers with the kidnapped children.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:22.840
For the first time in an MCU movie, we see
kids take on the bad guys, and it is pure,

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:24.330
thrilling wish fulfillment.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.320
As the song "November Rain" kicks into gear,
the kids strike back.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.021
Lightning erupts from their eyes and weapons
as they defeat their captors and Thor does

00:07:31.021 --> 00:07:32.021
battle with Gorr.

00:07:32.021 --> 00:07:37.120
For anyone who fell in love with superheroes
at a young age, this is a dream come true.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.490
Like the opening, the ending of "Thor: Love
and Thunder" is surprisingly lacking in the

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.150
epic battle sequence department.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:46.129
Marvel films have a habit of ending with two
superpowered beings punching each other into

00:07:46.129 --> 00:07:48.470
exhaustion, and the one left standing is the
victor.

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:52.789
But "Love and Thunder" begins its climax that
way before careening into uncharted territory

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:54.020
for the MCU.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.210
Gorr reaches Eternity.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.870
Jane is succumbing to her illness, and Thor
doesn't know quite what to do.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:02.539
Losing the love of his life and witnessing
her make the ultimate heroic sacrifice, he

00:08:02.539 --> 00:08:04.389
decides to open his heart to Gorr.

00:08:04.389 --> 00:08:07.949
As a result, Gorr doesn't wish for the end
of the gods, like he'd planned.

00:08:07.949 --> 00:08:10.199
Instead, Eternity brings his daughter back.

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:14.180
Thor, inspired by the villain's drive to honor
his daughter at all costs, proves to him that

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.240
the reason for living has always been love.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.300
If you're tired of loud, raucous, and tedious
finales, this ending should come as a breath

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.490
of fresh air, and is well worth a second viewing.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:26.689
At the very least, it should help explain
to any confused viewers why the film is called

00:08:26.689 --> 00:08:28.749
"Love and Thunder."

00:08:28.749 --> 00:08:31.449
Everyone knows to stick around during the
credits of a Marvel movie.

00:08:31.449 --> 00:08:35.050
Since Nick Fury introduced himself to Tony
Stark at the end of the original "Iron Man,"

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:38.709
it has become a tradition with each installment
to stay in your seat and see what tease they

00:08:38.709 --> 00:08:40.060
have in store for the next movie.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:43.050
In the case of "Thor: Love and Thunder," we
get two scenes.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:46.470
One lets us know that Jane made it to Valhalla
where she is greeted by Heimdall.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.630
The other sets up a very exciting antagonist
for the next film.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:53.519
The scene begins with a wounded Zeus lamenting
people's lack of interest in the gods.

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:56.270
He can remember a time when humans feared
and worshiped them.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.660
Now, all they care about are superheroes.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.340
Fed up with this fall from grace, he vows
to make them fear the gods once again.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:06.380
The suggestion that a new "Thor" movie featuring
gods terrorizing humanity is cool enough,

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.260
but the final reveal is the real kicker.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:12.450
It turns out that Zeus wasn't simply talking
to himself — he was giving orders to his

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:13.810
son Hercules.

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:18.130
Then we see that Brett Goldstein, who played
Roy Kent in "Ted Lasso," is playing the legendary

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.580
son of Zeus.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:23.180
Watching the son of Odin and the son of Zeus
duke it out is going to be a blast, and this

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.339
scene builds the hype perfectly.

